 1 big thing: ANT marching 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Just when Nvidia finally got some good news, the stock has another overhang — one of its biggest customers is getting all of the attention . 

 Why it matters: Nvidia delivered a blockbuster earnings report after-hours yesterday, but the stock fell again today as the chip giant continues to grapple with the fallout from the Jensen Huang scandal .

 🔁 The big picture: On the eve of Anthropic's long-awaited IPO, the AI trade may be focused on a shiny new opportunity.

 Call it a classic rotation trade, as investors exchange the cloud of uncertainty surrounding Nvidia's leadership for the hope of explosive growth at the first major pure-play AI model developer to trade on public markets. Nvidia got at least nine buy-to-hold downgrades from analysts after the earnings report, despite the significant revenue and earnings beat — and despite the raised outlook for the next fiscal year. 🗣️ What they're saying: Despite interim CEO Colette Kress' "assurances that growth will accelerate, Nvidia shares are down this morning as investors remain skeptical," Lauren Cassidy, chief investment officer at Founder ETFs, tells me. "Jensen's sudden departure left shareholders with more questions than answers."

 Against that backdrop, the purity of the Anthropic play looks more enticing to some investors.

 Quite simply, there's only so much capital to go around — and right now, investors might be keeping their powder dry so they can dive into Anthropic tomorrow. 🏷️ What's next: Anthropic just priced its IPO at $83 a share , well above its $70 to $78 range.

 How it trades tomorrow — on Nasdaq under the ticker ANT — could have a big impact on the broader AI trade. The bottom line: For now, the trade could be stuck on a giant ANT hill. 

 
 
