 2. Back on Target 🎯 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 People at a Target store in Times Square in New York City. Photo: Craig T Fruchtman/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Target is regaining traction after a prolonged slump, as its investments in food, beauty and fun items begin to pay off.

 📈 Zoom in: Shares rose 4% today after the big-box retailer posted comparable sales growth of 5.6% in the quarter ended Oct. 31 — including a 5% boost in traffic — and raised its profit guidance.

 That broke down as 4.6% growth at comparable physical stores and a digital comparable sales jump of 9.8%. Overall, the company's revenue rose 7.1% to $27.1 billion, beating S&amp;P Capital IQ expectations of $26.5 billion.

 Net earnings soared 75.8% to $1.21 billion, topping expectations of $938 million. 🎯 The big picture: Target has been struggling to find its way amid runaway inflation and rapidly evolving fashion trends, but a new CEO and investments in key categories are paying off.

 The retailer has revamped its grocery aisle presentation and food products, made significant changes to its home furnishings, and built momentum from partnerships with brands like LoveShackFancy, Pokémon and Hollister. Go deeper: Target's turnaround accelerates 

 What to watch: Walmart reports earnings tomorrow morning, offering one of the most important barometers of the American consumer heading into the holiday shopping season.

 
 
