 4. 🕔 After hours: The GTA productivity dip 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 A screenshot of Grand Theft Auto VI featuring the game's main characters, Lucia Caminos and Jason Duval. Photo: Courtesy of Rockstar Games

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Grand Theft Auto VI is going live at midnight, and the madness may be unlike anything we've seen before, Axios' Herb Scribner writes .

 Why it matters: GTA VI has been in development for roughly 13 years and is shaping up to be the biggest cultural phenomenon of 2026. Millions of Americans are thinking about skipping work to play it.

 The Take-Two Interactive-owned Rockstar Games release has already netted nearly $1.5 billion in sales, according to Sensor Tower estimates , with nearly 15 million digital pre-order copies sold across Xbox and PlayStation devices. 🔫 The big picture: Grand Theft Auto lets players roam a fictional city, steal cars and cause chaos — a satire of modern American life. Since debuting in 1997, it's become one of the world's biggest entertainment franchises.

 Past editions have been set in cities modeled on Los Angeles, New York and Miami. This version returns to Miami — named Vice City in the game. The last installment, Grand Theft Auto V, came out in 2013 and made $1 billion in three days , faster than any movie or game before it. More than a decade later, people are still playing it. 🍿 What they're saying: "If you took the Super Bowl, a blockbuster movie premiere and Christmas morning and rolled them all up into one, this is what you get," Justin Kenna, CEO of entertainment and tech company GameSquare, tells Axios.

 "Even people who don't play video games know about it because it's become such a massive cultural phenomenon." 🛜 What to watch: The debut is expected to be so big that internet providers are already sounding the alarm about global bandwidth and traffic disruptions as fans begin pre-downloads, Axios' Sara Fischer writes .

 
 
